Date: Wed, 13 Jan 93 12:29:42 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: NEWS/BRAIN: Making Your Dendrites Grow

The following news item came across my "Xpress" news service today. It
may have some relevance to Extropians, but is probably redundant, as
we all spend much of our time in intellectual matters.


Human brain improves with use

By Louise Yarnall
Copley News Service



   LOS ANGELES A team of UCLA researchers has found that the human
brain improves with use.

   The landmark study shows that stimulation actually changes the
brain's anatomy and may enhance mental functioning over a lifetime.

   The researchers found that dendrites, the nerve cell structures
that receive impulses through branch-like tendrils, grow longer the
more they are used. Dendrites are just one aspect of the complex
cellular communication system that makes the brain work. Scientists
theorize that longer dendrites process more information.

   It is the first time such a study has been conducted on human
brains and it corresponds with related animal research.

   "Brains change all the time and very quickly," said Bob Jacobs, a
postdoctoral scholar in the UCLA departments of pharmacology and
applied linguistics. "Just learning something, that changes the
brain. You can actually see an anatomical change in connections in
less than a minute. It's a very dynamic system."

   In the five-year study, researchers studied portions of the brains
of 20 deceased subjects. All were neurologically normal and belonged
to right-handed individuals. Some of the subjects had less than a high
school education, some were high school graduates, and some had a
university education. The tissue had been donated by next-of-kin or
through wills.

   The researchers focused on one tiny segment of the brain called
Wernicke's area, which is involved in language comprehension. It is
just above and behind the left ear.

   By a painstaking process, researchers counted out and measured
about 400 dendrites from the tissue samples.

   Each dendrite took about a half hour to an hour to isolate.
Researchers traced over each one, reproducing its three-dimensional
shape, Jacobs said. Then each dendrite tracing was fed into a computer
that measured them in micra or millionths of a meter.

   The study showed that people with higher educational levels and
intellectually-challenging jobs had the longer dendrites. People with
only a high school education, or less, and who worked in more physical
jobs, had shorter dendrites.

   The researchers also found that female subjects had longer
dendrites than men. They are not sure why, but it could relate to
previous findings that women are more adept in verbal matters.

   The study was conducted by Jacobs, Matthew Schall, a statistician
in the Office of Academic Computing, and Arnold Scheibel, director of
the UCLA Brain Research Institute. The results were published in the
January issue of The Journal of Comparative Neurology.

   Although the sample was small, Jacobs said he feels the results of
his study are solid. He said it demonstrates that neglect does not
just have behavioral consequences, but physical consequences. It is a
sign that activity enhances the brain's functioning, he said.

   "This is all within the realm of being reasonable," he said. "It
doesn't mean people should go out and try to read the whole contents
of the Santa Monica Library."

   He also said the research provides an important link between human
and animal research. Past brain studies have been conducted mostly on
rats and occasionally on cats and monkeys.

   "There's been 30 years of research in non-human animals that has
led researchers to believe that the brain is responsive to the
environment," Jacobs said. "But it's difficult extrapolating that to
humans. This is the first study that has attempted to do that."

   Jacobs said he would like to continue his research using a larger
sample and where he knew much more about the individuals and their
lives. He discussed comparing brain samples of people who spoke
different languages, or people who had healthy brains compared to
people who had brains afflicted by some disease, such as Alzheimer's.

   "There are so many things that go into individuals' lives. There's
no way of keeping track. Everything affects the brain, and it's the
sum total of their activities," he said. "The fact that everybody's
brain is different is, I guess, what makes life interesting."

   ) 1991 Copley News Service

