Date: Wed, 13 Jan 93 12:51:41 PST
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: Re:  Missing Gray Goo explained

Hal points out that an atomic-level simulation of a system will be
less efficient, in materials or time, than the system itself.  This
suggests that we may wish to build our VRs around other primitives
than atoms.  This will restrict our ability to make the virtual
world act like World Prime, but we may not want to do that anyway.

What to use as primitives is a dificult question.  Building out of
rules is an example of primitives which are not spatial, but is
unlikely to be practical for any but toy worlds.  Text adventures
often build out of objects with rules attached; the lack of detail
in behavior seems to be a result of working at too high a level,
resulting in objects that have to be told how to do everything. 

Present VR systems frequently use geometric primitives such as
triangles.  I consider this to be symptomatic of an overly visual
orientation.  Having built objects out of dumb triangles,
programmers have no choice other than to go the text-adventure route
and start hanging rules off of these gloms of triangles.

That said, I must admit that I don't know what would be a good
primitive either.  In the spatial/geometric class, it might be
interesting to try a sphere-and-spring architecture.  This would
allow more mechanical know-how to be built into objects --
bounciness, breakability, flexibility, cleavage planes -- though it
would probably introduce its own peculiar artifacts.  Further
afield, building out of constraints is a possibilty, but I'm not
familiar with work done in this area.

