Date: Wed, 13 Jan 93 13:39:00 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Dyson Spheres

Mark Pickens writes:

>Maybe the reason we haven't seen stars shifting to infra-red is that only a
>fraction of a particular star's output is necessary to power the largest
>computer possible to build with the available matter in that solar system.
>If everyone's on a VR system operating at maximum efficiency, the extra
>energy could be superfluous.

The speed at which a reversible computer can be run is a function of
the entropy it can waste, the negentropy it has available to it.  The
more entropy, the faster it can run.  So there's no upper limit to the
fraction of star output that could be made use of.  

>Does anyone know the theoretical maximum information storage density?  I was
>told once that individual iron atoms might be able to be used as on/off
>switches.

An upper bound is the entropy of a black hole.  In units where
G=k=h=c=1,  Mass=Radius, and Entropy = Mass^2.  Were you able to store
more bits than this in such a mass, then this mass would be stable
against collapse to a black hole, since such collapse would violate
the 2nd law.  But singularity theorems imply such collapse is
possible.  Of course the practical max may be much less than this bound.

Robin 

