I don't have a citation, but it is true of everyone that I
have ever talked to about their driving. Especially men.

I have a simple suggestion for improving one's chance of
living while using cars without the expense of buying a 5000
pound Mercedes, and it is fun too:

Take a professional racing drivers course. There are
lots at nearly any road course. (Taking the NASCAR course at
Charlotte would be fun, but not very applicable). The racing
schools come to all the major tracks, and there is a track
within six hours or so of 95% of the US population.

The course will teach you how to drive the car at the
98% level of what the car can do. Cars are capable of far more
than the self defined "better than average" driver can dream
of. You can't learn this on the street, you have to have a
track and an instructor.

You will probably slow down your street driving a bit after
you get some track time. You simply realise that no amount of
street squirrel action gets the adrenaline going like track
time.

As a measure of how little you use on the street: I raced roadraced 
motorcycles (paved road courses) for ten years and I learned a lot. 

I had raced in the dirt for years before I tried
roadracing. I considered myseld a hot street rider. My first
roadrace was in the amatuer classes at Daytona. I practiced,
and learned. In the race, on the sixth lap, I was going as
fast as humanly possible through turn 3 (the very fast left
handed sweaper in the infield) when I was lapped by a rider
who passed me on the outside with at least a 30 MPH speed
differential. Same bike, same tires, same track. He simply
used what the bike could do.


So take a class and learn how to drive. You may not use the
knowledge often, but it is nice insurance. And it is a lot of
fun.

