Date: Wed, 13 Jan 93 15:06:36 -0800
From: David Jevans <jevans@apple.com>
Subject: Re:  Missing Gray Goo explained

Ei writes:

> Present VR systems frequently use geometric primitives such as triangles.

> In the spatial/geometric class, it might be interesting to try a
> sphere-and-spring architecture.

Triangles are used as the representation for the rendering system.  If you
build a world simply of geometric descriptions then you can fly around in
it and perhaps animate parts of it, but that's it.

The base representation may be polygons or parametric surfaces or implicit
surfaces, but they are usually broken down into triangles for rendering.

You touch on dynamics (springs and masses).  This is very simple dynamics.
To actually implement friction (esp. sliding friction) is difficult,
and very difficult in anything near real-time.

But these are all toy problems considered with representing anything
approaching reality.  CAD visualization of an airplane wing for example
is very complicated.  But even this level of simulation is nothing compared
to actual molecular or atomic simulation.  It all depends to what level
you want to simulat things.  Current simulation technology would have
us living in a cartoon existence of infinitely-thin walls, would have us
penetrating walls due to the granularity of the time base for solving the
dynamics, etc.

If we ever can build a decent simulation of World-prime (neat term!)
and upload into it, wouldn't we then know enough about it to be able to
plan and predict rather than rely on a free market?  After all, it sort
of implies that we have transcended this reality and are able to capture it
and simulate it, therefore don't we understand it?  This is sort of the
classic argument between using dynamic or kinematic methods for animation.
If the end results are the same, why bother going to all the trouble
of building a dynamic simulation?  Why not animate instead of simulate?

A possibility of uploading is that some corporeal person will find it to be
very beneficial to have a little virtual world that runs at accelerated
speed.  They could simulate conditions, present them to the uploadees,
and see what they figure out, see how they react.  Then use that data for
commercial benefit.  They could even put you in there, let you think and
invent etc at super-speed, and then "steal" your inventions for use in
the "real" world.  Or use it as a little zoo.  Make broadcast TV from your
antics in V-land.  As long as they keep you disconnected from the net
it would be a real prison.  There could come to be a black market in
e-personae, virtual slavery.

:-)

