Date: Wed, 13 Jan 1993 15:12:38 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Re: I&A Doug's Hip PC

Caveat: On this list, I typically discuss future business
possibilities, not present efforts.  In this case talented people 
are already working on HipPC, and no offense or pretensions at 
expertise are intended with this "back seat driving" :-)

The social stigma problem is quite serious, IMHO.  Note that 
even plain-old corrective glasses have often carried a stigma.
Sony Walkmans succeeded with extensive marketing, but even now
are _verbotten_ in many circumstances (eg, when driving here in
Oregon).  Cellular phones succeeded because they were overwhelmingly
useful, and the business grew in a niche where social stigma has
little effect (inside the private car).  Once cellular succeeded
in the car, it started breaking into other areas, like making
phone calls in restaraunts, which many consider rude behavior.
If the cellular phones had first been targeted to public use, they 
would likely have been a flop.

So, one marketing strategy could be heavy specialization in
areas where usefulness is more importat than fashion, eg the
following niches:

* police, fire, ambulance personel
* use in private car, if driving safety can be ensured
* use in private boats and planes.  For example, I know a writer 
  who uses a Private-Eye-like device with his portable PC while
  cruising in his Beechcraft.
* use on mass transit: airplanes, buses, trains
* farmers in their tractor cabs, to tap in to weather reports,
  commodity information, farm planning on computer, etc.
* military & espionage

A second market strategy could be extensive advertising, with the
goal of making public use, at least in planes, buses, trains, etc.
socially acceptable.  Especially effective might be product placements
in movies and sitcoms, showing that fashionable lawyers and businessmen
use the device.

I am also concerned that HipPC/Private Eye introduces too many new 
features at once.  Not only must users overcome the fear of social stigma,
they must also relearn how to type.  Introducing chording via normal 
portable PCs might be a better strategy.  The belt chording device
would then be hooked up to a LCD notebook display, which is more portable
for having lost its keyboard.  Chording seems advantagious for machine 
operators who must keep one hand occupied, and that might be a good
niche for initial marketing.

