Date: Wed, 13 Jan 93 15:20:47 -0800
From: David Jevans <jevans@apple.com>
Subject: Re:  Living in alien VR

Robin Hanson writes:
> You seem to think that it somehow takes fewer bits to describe a
> quantum state of the universe that to describe a classical state.

I'm not suggesting that there is a quantum state being described.  I'm
suggesting that a classical state is what is being simulated, and that
where the simulation runs out of precision is where uncertainty enters.
_We_ invented quantum mechanics to explain and predict the behaviour
of this underdetermined system.

Just like when you simulate Newtonian dynamics you run into precision
problems describing physical location and time slices.  The end result is
uncertainty.  At any time interval or spatial resolution below the precision
of the machine you can't predict the location or behaviour of the object.
So you put up with the inaccuracies, make assumptions (like "when two
surfaces are within some epsilon distance from each other, consider them to
be touching"), or revert to statistical methods.  Just like when describing
the universe.

I just thought of this:  Pi*r^2 (pi-r-squared) doesn't work in physical
reality!  For instance, when you get down to the sub-atomic level of
describing any physical manifestation of a circle, it's perimeter can't be
said to lie _exactly_ on the circle, due to quantum effects.  Does anyone
know of a study of quantum geometry?

-dj

