Date: Wed, 13 Jan 93 16:11:46 PST
From: hkhenson@cup.portal.com
Subject: Re: Avoiding death/thrills

Re avoiding death, I have yet to skydive or ride a donorcycle at 120 
mph, but I have: done technical mountain climbing, including repelling 
off the Red Wall limestone in the Grand Canyon; explored some serious 
vertical caves; fired some tons of explosives over a period of many 
years; built and fired cannons and solid and liquid fuel rockets; and 
long ago I used to replace lights on the top of radio towers. 

More recently I have been seriously involved with that most extropian 
of activities, cryonics, including helping with the surgery on the 
last AIDS case Alcor did.  (Alcor has done three AIDS case, and I have 
been at two of them.  I am training to be their backup surgeon in dire 
circumstances.)  Last weekend Alcor had a defunct animal left over 
from a blood washout training/research effort.  Before it went to 
disposal, I took a "whack" at it. 

I wrote up how you go about putting in heart bypass plumbing--which is 
necessary before perfusing patients with cryoprotectives.  Thought the 
"wet work" phase of cryonics might interest a few of you.  The 
squeamish should skip the rest now. 

1.  Prep and drape.  Clean a large area around midline on the chest 
with Betadine.  Attach drapes with towel clamps leaving a 2 inch wide 
space on the midline. 

2.  Open incision through skin with #11 blade.  Cut down to bone with 
electrocautery.  This will leave you and the entire area smelling of 
incinerated meat for the next two days.  However, burning through does 
seal up the wound edges so they don't leak too much. 

3.  Cut through bone on midline with a Striker (or Sarns) saw.  Some 
of you may know what a Striker saw looks like, since they are often 
used to open casts.  When through, open chest about six inches with a 
retractor.  Rub bone wax on the marrow surfaces of the bone to keep it 
from leaking too much. 

4.  Locate heart and remove excess fat from the surface of the 
pericardium.  *Carefully* split the pericardium on the front surface. 

5.  "Tent" the pericardium by sewing it in four or 6 places to the 
skin edges of the wound.  This raises the heart and makes the next 
phase much easier to do. 

6.  Put a purse string suture on the aorta.  Using a monofilament type 
suture with half inch long semicircular needles attached, sew a loop 
*in* (not through!) the wall of the aorta.  The aorta is fully four 
inches down from the chest wall, so use a needle holder (which looks 
like a blunt pair of hemostats) to make the stitches.  Leave 5-6 
inches of suture on each end, and thread the ends through a 2 inch 
chunk of stiff red rubber tube.  Put a hemostat on the tube leaving 
the suture loose. 

7.  Fill the arterial catheter with saline and clamp it with a tubing 
occluder clamp. 

8.  Make an incision in the aorta, using a number 11 blade.  Start 
most of the way over on one side of the suture circle with the blunt 
side of the blade to the outside.  Try not to go entirely *through* 
the aorta. 

9.  Insert the arterial cannula into the slit.  Tighten the purse 
strings by pulling back on the suture and pushing down on the red 
rubber.  Clamp them tight.  Secure cannula with basket tie or other 
clips to *be sure* it will not come out under pressure. 

10.  Repeat 6-9 for the much smaller pressure measuring cannula. 

11.  Clamp off the tip of the right atrium with an appropriate 
instrument.  (A large curved hemostat will do in a real pinch.)  Put 
purse strings around the heart side of the clamp.  Four stitches will 
do.  Thread through red rubber and clamp as above. 

12.  Cut off the exposed atrium tip.  Fill the venous cannula, release 
clamp, grab the edges with 2-3 hemostats, and insert cannula.  May 
need to open up the hole a little with reversed action on some Mayo 
scissors. 

13.  Pull strings up and clamp inside red rubber tube.  Secure with 
basket tie or better. 

14.  For an extra thrill, try this with an AIDS patient--and stick a 
needle through your glove. 

Keith Henson

