I have to agree with Perry on this one, though I certainly think Dave
has a point. And when I was still working for Intel, I used to say the
same kinds about Intel's short-term viewpoint.

In my case, falling into this view (and I'm not saying Dave has
"fallen in" to anything...these comments apply to me alone) was an
easy slam against things I didn't like at Intel.

In my years away from day to day work, I've gotten a better view--it
might be said that _my own_ view used to be parochial and short-term,
which is an irony. I now see that most successful American
corporations are in fact very interested in long term matters (for the
most part).

Supposedly, Akio Morita of Sony--a company I respect and buy a lot of
products from--visited Wall Street (maybe even Perry's area?) and
watched the deals going down. He pronounced that this atmosphere
typified the American infatuation with the ultra short term view...he
claimed the focus was not on the next quarter, but on the next 15
minutes! (Note: I may have some of the details wrong...this came out
of the infamous "The Japan That Can Say 'No'" report from a few years
back.) 

Well, gosh, what did he expect? Money deals and trades in NYC will
look a lot like the same deals in Tokyo...this says very little about
multi-year efforts to develop new processes, to build new factories,
and to launch new products.

Here are some miscellaneous facts to consider:

* Intel is now the world's largest and most profitable semiconductor
company. Earning were announced today, far in excess of expectations.
Japan is hurting, having put nearly all their eggs in the dynamic RAM
business...and thus missing out on microprocessors, programmable
logic, personal computers, workstations, and so on. And the Koreans
are closing in on them in DRAMS!

(By the way, Intel stock jumped $9 today to close at $110. Just a
month ago it was at $70.)

* Japan uses very few PCs, far fewer per capita than the U.S. uses.
This cripples their home-grown software business, among other things.
(A friend of mine has spent the past 6 years as Intel's technology
guru in Tsukuba, Japan's "science city." He reports they are getting
increasingly worried that their carefully planned (emphasis on
"planned") approach is failing.)

* Apple, Sun, H-P, Intel, etc., all have aggressive long-range product
plans. I can't speak for Apple (as Dave J. can), but I know that the
various CPU chips by the others are all long-range (2-3 year) efforts,
with an overall planning horizon of 3-5 years.

* Even car makers are doing well. Ford has 5 out of the top 10 selling
vehicles in the U.S. I just bought a Ford Explorer 4x4 truck, a top
seller. It was built completely in Louisville, Kentucky (is that where
Krzys, from Poland, is from?). The quality is spectacular.

And so it goes.

Imagine how much better things would be if capital gains taxes were
reduced to zero, as they are in Japan and several European countries.
And imagine if other government interference was lessened.

Talk of "industrial policy" is disturbing. MITI wasn't able to pick
winners in Japan (MITI urged Honda to stay out of the car business,
and pressured Sony not to enter the consumer electronics business.
MITI also architected the move into DRAMS and the flopperoo "Fifth
Generation" boondoggle, which helped Japan to miss out on
workstations, hypercubes, Connection Machines, neural nets, and fuzzy
logic.)

A recent issue of "The Economist" discusses this business of
governments using coercive powers to "pick winners." Outsmarting the
market is usually a lost cause.

Both Clinton/Gore's talk of a "national research network" and
Drexler's recent efforts to cozy up to Gore and other bureaucrats are
alarming. (We've discussed the implications of a "National Nanotech
Initiative" at least a couple of times on this list.)

Anyway, I'd be interested to hear more of Dave's views on short term
outlooks...it seems like a pretty good topic for Extropians and the
"long view." But, like Perry, I suspect things are working pretty
well.

-Tim May


